-- title:
--   Lemma 3.10 (corrected: $\mu^\star(x)>0$) — $\sum_{x'\in\mathcal S^j}\mathbb 1\{x'\preceq x\}/\hat\mu(x')$ is a martingale with mean $N$
-- statement:
--   Under the hypotheses of Lemma 3.9, fix $x\in\Sigma^n$ with $\mu^\star(x)>0$ and set $\Phi_x(\mathcal S)=\sum_{x'\in\mathcal S}\mathbb 1\{x'\preceq x\}/\hat\mu(x')$ for a multiset $\mathcal S$ of particles. Along the inner loop of Algorithm 3,
--
--   1. $(\Phi_x(\mathcal S^j))_{j\ge0}$ is a martingale with respect to $(\mathscr F^j)_{j\ge0}$: for every reachable state, the expected value of $\Phi_x$ after one more iteration equals its current value;
--   2. in particular, for every $j\ge0$,
--   $$
--   \mathbb E\Bigl[\sum_{x'\in\mathcal S^j}\frac{\mathbb 1\{x'\preceq x\}}{\hat\mu(x')}\Bigr]=N.
--   $$
--
--   Here $(\mathscr F^j)$ is the filtration generated by all random variables of Algorithm 3 up to iteration $j$, not the class $\mathcal F^j$. The identity yields the unbiasedness of the terminal particle system (Lemma 3.11).
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - correction: the lemma for each $x\in\Sigma^n$ → for each $x\in\Sigma^n$ with $\mu^\star(x)>0$; reason: as printed it is false when $\mu^\star(x)=0$: no particle ever reaches such an $x$, so $\Phi_x(\mathcal S^J)=0\ne N=\Phi_x(\mathcal S^0)$; example $n=1$, $\Sigma=\{0,1\}$, $\mathcal F$ the two point masses, $\mu^\star=\delta_1=\hat\mu$, $R=1$, $\delta=1/10$, $x=0$, $C_N\ge1$ ($N\ge7$); the proof divides by $\hat\mu(w_j)=0$; Lemma 3.11 is unaffected
--   - encoding: martingale with respect to the filtration $(\mathscr F^j)$ of all random variables up to iteration $j$ → one-step identity $\mathbb E[\,\cdot\,(\text{next state})\mid\text{state }j]=\,\cdot\,(\text{state }j)$ at every state of a path of positive probability, the transition being `alg3Step`; reason: the inner loop is a Markov chain on (particles, class, cache) driven by fresh randomness, so the two statements are the same
--   - encoding: $n$ a positive integer, $\mathcal F$ finite (Algorithm 3's input) → $1\le n$ and $2\le|\mathcal F|$; reason: Line 4 queries strings of length $1$; at $|\mathcal F|=1$ the formula for $N$ contains $\log 0$; only excludes undefined cases
--   - reading: "We also adopt the convention that $\mu(\emptyset)=1$" (p.18) → hypothesis $\hat\mu(\emptyset)=1$; reason: the proofs of Lemmas 3.10 and 3.15 use $\hat\mu(\emptyset)=1$; read as part of the standing setup
--   - reading: Algorithm 3 ("lexicographically first", the while loop) → `alg3Step` with Mathlib's lexicographic order on lists; the loop run for $L=N+2\bar R(\lfloor\log_2|\mathcal F|\rfloor+1)$ iterations of a kernel that is the identity on terminal states; reason: by Lemma 3.9 the loop ends within $L$ iterations, so this is the paper's loop
--   - reading: Algorithm 2 (Line 11's "for some $y$"; "If $\xi=1$, add $w_{i,t}$ to $\mathcal T$; set $\mathcal G^{\mathrm{ret}}\leftarrow\dots$") → `alg2`: the least violating $y$ in the order of $\Sigma$; both actions under $\xi=1$; coins clamped to $[0,1]$; a trial with undefined $\bar\eta(\cdot\mid v)$ ends with nothing added; reason: the printed line leaves $y$ unspecified; clamping and the fallback act only on unreachable states
-- source:
--   Golowich, Moitra, Rohatgi, The Power of Test-Time Training for Approximate Sampling, arXiv:2606.11437 (version of November 3, 2026), Lemma 3.10, p.26 (PDF p.28)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_TTTSampling_Assumption31
import Definitions.Def_TTTSampling_tvDist
import Definitions.Def_TTTSampling_alg3Path

namespace TTTSampling

/-- **Lemma 3.10** (corrected: `µ⋆(x) > 0`). For each `x ∈ Σⁿ` and `j ≥ 0`, `(Σ_{x′∈𝒮^j} 1{x′ ⪯ x}/µ̂(x′))_{j≥0}` is a
martingale with respect to `(ℱ^j)_{j≥0}`, and in particular
`𝔼[Σ_{x′∈𝒮^j} 1{x′ ⪯ x}/µ̂(x′)] = N`.

Golowich, Moitra, Rohatgi, arXiv:2606.11437 (version of November 3, 2026), Lemma 3.10, printed
p.26 (PDF p.28). `(ℱ^j)` here is the filtration of p.26 (all random variables of Algorithm 3 up
to iteration `j`), not the class `ℱ^j`.

**Formalization Note**
* The inner loop is a Markov chain on states (particles, class, cache) driven by fresh
  randomness, so the martingale property is the one-step identity
  `𝔼[Φ_x(𝒮^{j+1}) | state j] = Φ_x(𝒮^j)` at every state of a path of positive probability (first
  clause; `alg3Step` is the transition). The second clause is the displayed identity, for every
  `j` (`sAt` stays at the last state beyond the path, as `𝒮^j := 𝒮^J` for `j > J`).
* `x ∈ Σⁿ` is a string `Fin n → Σ`, as a list `List.ofFn x`. Standing data as in Lemma 3.9.
* **Correction.** `x` is restricted to the support of `µ⋆` (`µ⋆(x) > 0`). As printed ("for each
  x ∈ Σⁿ") the lemma is false when `µ⋆(x) = 0`: no particle ever reaches such an `x` (its coin
  `µ̂(x)/(M·…)` is `0`), so `Φ_x(𝒮^J) = 0` while `Φ_x(𝒮^0) = N`. Example: `n = 1`, `Σ = {0,1}`,
  `ℱ` the two point masses, `µ⋆ = δ_1`, `µ̂ = µ⋆`, `R = 1`, `δ = 1/10`, `x = 0`, `C_N ≥ 1`
  (`N ≥ 7`). The proof's step `𝔼[occ(w_j, 𝒯_j)]/µ̂(w_j) = 1/µ̂(z_j)` divides by `µ̂(w_j) = 0` there.
  Lemma 3.11, the only use of Lemma 3.10, is unaffected (both sides are `0` when `µ⋆(x) = 0`). -/
theorem lemma_3_10 (C_N : ℕ) {α : Type} [Fintype α] [LinearOrder α] {n : ℕ}
    (F : Finset (PMF (Fin n → α))) (R δ : ℝ) (μh : List α → NNReal) (μs : PMF (Fin n → α))
    (hn : 1 ≤ n) (hF : 2 ≤ F.card) (hμs : μs ∈ F) (hA : Assumption31 μs μh R)
    (h0 : μh [] = 1) (hR : 1 ≤ R) (hδ : 0 < δ) (hδ1 : δ < 1) (x : Fin n → α)
    (hx : μs x ≠ 0) :
    (∀ p ∈ (alg3Path C_N R δ F μh).support, ∀ j : ℕ, j < pathLen C_N F.card R δ →
      expect (alg3Step R μh (stAt p j)) (fun s => Phi μh (List.ofFn x) s.S) =
        Phi μh (List.ofFn x) (sAt p j)) ∧
    ∀ j : ℕ, expect (alg3Path C_N R δ F μh) (fun p => Phi μh (List.ofFn x) (sAt p j)) =
      Npart C_N F.card R δ := by sorry

end TTTSampling
